# 7. Vehicle Lookup

Vehicle lookup is the primary entry point.

## 7.1 Input

The user should be able to enter a VIN or supported vehicle identifier.

Example:

```text
Vehicle identifier

[ 1HGCM82633A004352                  ]

                         [ View history ]
```

The input should provide:

* clear label
* validation
* useful error messages
* keyboard accessibility
* loading state

Do not make the user interact with a decorative search component before accessing the vehicle.

---

# 8. Vehicle Context Header

Once a vehicle is loaded, its identity should remain visible.

Example:

```text
Vehicle history

VIN
1HGCM82633A004352

Reports
18

Observed history
2024-02-12 → 2026-08-14

[ Add report ]
```

The header should provide context without becoming a dashboard.

---

# 9. Vehicle History Timeline

The timeline is the primary visual component of the application.

It represents what was reported and when.

Example:

```text
2026-08-14
MECHANIC

Transmission hesitation during 2→3 shift.

────────────────────────────────────────

2026-06-20
OWNER

Transmission operating normally.

────────────────────────────────────────

2026-03-02
INSPECTOR

Minor transmission fluid leak observed.
```

Each event should expose:

* date
* source type
* report text
* report status
* relevant issue/category when available

---

# 10. Timeline Ordering

Default ordering:

```text
Newest
  ↓
Oldest
```

Users should be able to understand how the current assessment developed.

The timeline must preserve chronological facts.

Do not rewrite historical reports according to the current assessment.

For example, if a later mechanic report indicates a transmission issue, an earlier owner report stating that the transmission operated normally must remain unchanged.

---

# 11. Timeline Event Design

Timeline events should be visually distinguishable without excessive styling.

Recommended hierarchy:

```text
DATE
Source type

Original report text

Issue/category
```

Example:

```text
14 Aug 2026
Mechanic

"Noticeable hesitation between second and third gear."

Transmission
```

The original report text should remain accessible.

---

# 12. Claims vs Findings

The interface must distinguish between:

### Claim

Something reported by a source.

Example:

> "Transmission feels normal."

### Finding

A system-level interpretation supported by accumulated evidence.

Example:

> Transmission behavior shows repeated evidence of abnormal shifting.

The UI should never visually present a claim as an established fact.

---

# 13. Current Assessment

The current assessment should be presented separately from the raw timeline.

Example:

```text
TRANSMISSION

Elevated concern

3 independent reports
Observed across 99 days

[ Why this assessment? ]

Evidence confidence
86%
```

The assessment is a synthesis of the evidence.

It should not replace the evidence.

---

# 14. Assessment States

Assessment labels should remain limited and understandable.

Possible states:

```text
No significant evidence
Limited evidence
Moderate evidence
Elevated concern
Strong evidence
Conflicting evidence
```

The final vocabulary should remain aligned with the scoring model.

The frontend must not invent additional assessment categories independently.

---

# 15. Evidence Confidence

Evidence confidence should use progressive disclosure.

Collapsed:

```text
Evidence confidence
86%
```

Expanded:

```text
Evidence confidence

86%

Based on:
• 3 independent sources
• repeated observations
• source reliability history
• supporting and contradictory evidence
• observation span

This is an evidence-strength estimate,
not a probability or mechanical diagnosis.

[ How is this calculated? ]
```

The user should be able to inspect why the value exists.

---

# 16. Confidence Explanation

The frontend should provide the following explanation:

> Evidence confidence describes the strength of the available evidence supporting a finding. It is not a probability that a mechanical problem exists and is not a mechanical diagnosis.

The interface should not label the number:

```text
Probability
```

or:

```text
Chance of failure
```

---

# 17. Supporting Evidence

Supporting evidence should be directly inspectable.

Example:

```text
SUPPORTING EVIDENCE

3 reports support this finding

14 Aug 2026 · Mechanic
"Noticeable hesitation between second and third gear."

08 Aug 2026 · Inspector
"Transmission exhibited delayed engagement."

02 Jun 2026 · Mechanic
"Customer reports intermittent shifting hesitation."
```

Each evidence item should link back to its originating report.

---

# 18. Contradictory Evidence

Contradictory evidence must remain visible.

Example:

```text
CONTRADICTORY EVIDENCE

Earlier reports described normal operation.

20 Jun 2026 · Owner
"Transmission operating normally."

This report conflicts with later observations
describing shifting hesitation.
```

The interface must not hide contradictory evidence simply because the current assessment has stronger support.

---

# 19. Unresolved Evidence

Evidence that cannot currently be corroborated or contradicted should be shown as unresolved.

Example:

```text
UNRESOLVED

04 Apr 2026 · Owner

"Occasional vibration at highway speeds."

No later independent observation currently
supports or contradicts this report.
```

This prevents unresolved claims from being presented as established findings.

---

# 20. Source History

Source reliability should be accessible from an evidence item.

Example:

```text
SOURCE RELIABILITY

Mechanic source

Estimated reliability
91%

Reports        18
Corroborated   14
Contradicted    2
Unresolved      2

This estimate is based on historical reporting
behavior. It is not a measure of professional
qualification.
```

The frontend must not describe this value as:

```text
Mechanic skill
```

or:

```text
Professional rating
```

---

# 21. Source Type

Supported source types:

```text
Owner
Buyer
Mechanic
Inspector
```

Use consistent labels throughout the application.

Avoid unnecessary visual badges.

A small source label is sufficient:

```text
14 Aug 2026 · Mechanic
```

---

# 22. Report Submission

The report submission workflow should be simple.

Example:

```text
Add report

Vehicle
1HGCM82633A004352

Source
[ Mechanic ▾ ]

Observation date
[ 14 Aug 2026 ]

Report

[ Describe what was observed...                 ]
[                                               ]
[                                               ]

                              [ Save report ]
```

The user should not need to understand:

* Hindsight
* embeddings
* retrieval
* source reliability formulas
* LLM processing
* evidence weights

Those are backend concerns.

---

# 23. Report Validation

Frontend validation should provide immediate feedback for obvious input errors.

Examples:

```text
Vehicle identifier is required.

Source type is required.

Observation date is required.

Report text cannot be empty.
```

The backend remains authoritative for validation.

Frontend validation must never replace backend validation.

---

# 24. Submission State

During submission:

```text
Saving report…

Processing historical evidence…
```

Avoid fake progress percentages.

Do not display:

```text
AI is thinking...
87%
```

unless the application actually has a measurable operation corresponding to that progress.

---

# 25. Successful Submission

After successful submission:

```text
Report added

The vehicle history has been updated.

[ View updated history ]
```

The assessment should then be recalculated by the backend.

---

# 26. Empty History

An empty vehicle history is different from a failed memory lookup.

Empty history:

```text
No history yet

No reports have been recorded for this vehicle.

[ Add first report ]
```

This means the system successfully checked the vehicle and found no stored history.

---

# 27. Hindsight Unavailable

If Hindsight cannot be reached, the frontend must not display:

```text
No history found
```

Instead:

```text
History temporarily unavailable

VeriCar could not retrieve the vehicle's stored
history.

Your report has not been interpreted as evidence
until historical memory can be retrieved.

[ Retry ]
```

This distinction is mandatory.

---

# 28. LLM Unavailable

If the LLM service fails but historical evidence and deterministic scoring are available:

```text
Assessment available

The evidence calculation completed successfully.

Automated explanation is temporarily unavailable.

[ View evidence ]
```

The interface should continue to expose:

* timeline
* evidence
* source history
* deterministic assessment

The LLM must not become a single point of failure for the entire interface.

---

# 29. Backend Error

Unexpected errors should be explicit.

Example:

```text
Something went wrong

VeriCar could not complete this request.

Request ID: 7f3c...

[ Try again ]
```

Do not expose stack traces, internal service names, API keys, or implementation details.

---

# 30. Loading States

Loading states should correspond to actual operations.

Examples:

```text
Loading vehicle history…
```

```text
Calculating evidence…
```

```text
Loading source history…
```

Use restrained skeletons or inline progress indicators.

Avoid full-screen animated loaders for ordinary API requests.

---

# 31. Responsive Design

The application must support:

* desktop
* tablet
* mobile

Desktop should use the available width for evidence comparison.

Mobile should prioritize chronological readability.

Desktop:

```text
┌──────────────────────┬──────────────────────┐
│ History              │ Current assessment   │
│                      │                      │
│ Timeline             │ Evidence confidence  │
│                      │                      │
│                      │ Supporting evidence  │
└──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┘
```

Mobile:

```text
Vehicle
↓
Assessment
↓
Confidence
↓
Timeline
↓
Supporting evidence
↓
Contradictions
```

Do not simply shrink the desktop layout.

---

# 32. Responsive Timeline

On small screens, timeline events should become a single readable column.

Avoid horizontal scrolling for primary evidence.

The user must be able to read:

* date
* source
* observation
* issue
* status

without manipulating the page horizontally.

---

# 33. Accessibility

The frontend must support:

* keyboard navigation
* visible focus states
* semantic HTML
* accessible form labels
* sufficient text contrast
* meaningful button names
* screen-reader-readable status messages
* logical heading hierarchy
* accessible expandable sections

Do not rely solely on color.

For example, contradictory evidence should not be represented only by red.

Use:

```text
Contradictory evidence
```

as text.

---

# 34. Typography

Typography should establish hierarchy rather than decoration.

Recommended hierarchy:

```text
Application title
Vehicle identifier
Section heading
Assessment label
Body text
Metadata
```

Avoid:

* oversized marketing typography
* all-caps paragraphs
* excessive font weights
* multiple decorative typefaces

Numbers such as evidence confidence may receive stronger typographic emphasis, but should remain proportional to the surrounding content.

---

# 35. Color System

Color should have semantic meaning.

Suggested roles:

```text
Primary text
Secondary text
Border/divider
Neutral background
Informational
Warning
Error
Success
```

Do not assign a different color to every category.

Assessment colors should not imply certainty beyond the underlying evidence.

---

# 36. Borders and Surfaces

Use borders and spacing to establish structure.

Preferred:

```text
Section heading
────────────────────────

Content
```

rather than:

```text
╭──────────────────────────╮
│  Section                 │
│                          │
│  Content                 │
╰──────────────────────────╯
```

Cards should be reserved for genuinely independent interactive or informational units.

---

# 37. Spacing

Spacing should establish hierarchy.

Use larger spacing between:

* major sections
* assessment and evidence
* timeline periods

Use smaller spacing between:

* metadata
* labels
* related values

Avoid excessive empty space that makes the application resemble a landing page.

---

